WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:03.630
- [Narrator] This is the
IBM Personal Computer.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:07.530
It was the most advanced
computing machine ever created.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.990
However, this isn't a
story about a computer,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.810
but a story about when a
computer stops working,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.520
and the man who created a
way to start it all over;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.551
the man who invented
Control + Alt + Delete.

00:00:19.551 --> 00:00:22.167
(chiming music)

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:23.170
(exciting music)

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.213
Let's get into it.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.340
- [Dave] Hi, I'm Dr. Dave Bradley,

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.830
but you can call me Dr. Dave.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:35.130
Back in 1980, I worked on
the IBM Personal Computer.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.560
My particular job on the IBM PC

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.511
was writing the basic input-output system.

00:00:40.511 --> 00:00:42.980
(clicking)

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.490
- [Narrator] Even with an elite team

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.217
of engineers and designers,
not everything goes smoothly

00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:48.963
when you're building a computer.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.700
- [Dave] We had programs
that ran most of the time,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.790
but when they failed the
only way to reset the system

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.407
was turn the power off,

00:00:58.407 --> 00:00:59.460
(clock ticking)
wait a while,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.200
turn the power back on,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.500
and it would go through
a very long self test.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:08.453
But the system might die
every five to 10 minutes.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.650
- [Narrator] What he needed
was a way to shortcut

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.260
the restart process.
(beeping)

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.880
- [Dave] One of the things we discussed

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.020
was putting a reset button on it.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.760
But if you put it on the system board,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.860
there was a chance that
you could hit it by mistake

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.092
and all your data gets lost.

00:01:25.092 --> 00:01:27.790
(upbeat electronic music)

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:31.272
So what we did was came up
with a three-key sequence

00:01:31.272 --> 00:01:33.520
that would reset the computer,

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.703
and you couldn't hit by mistake.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.380
A single Control key, a single Alt key,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.920
and then, all the way over
at the right-hand side,

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.143
a single Delete key.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.290
You hit that Control + Alt + Delete,

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.959
you're deleting everything that
you're working on right now

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:53.098
and starting new.

00:01:53.098 --> 00:01:56.410
I was also able to skip
over many of the tests,

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.120
so instead of taking a minute or two,

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.873
it was 10 or 15 seconds.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.650
But it wasn't a big deal at the time.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.680
It was like number 17 on the
list of 100 different things

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.043
I had to fix.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.870
- [Narrator] Even though
it was built only as

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.070
a development tool,

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:16.940
programmers began
incorporating the feature

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:18.463
into their applications.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.360
From there it was released into the wild,

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.304
but didn't immediately
reach pop culture status.

00:02:25.304 --> 00:02:26.560
(bomb whistling)
- [Dave] For years

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.300
it was no big deal.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.671
And then at the 20th
anniversary of the IBM PC,

00:02:31.671 --> 00:02:35.220
that's when Control + Alt + Delete

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.669
became sort of a cultural icon.

00:02:37.669 --> 00:02:39.660
(exploding)
(screeching)

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.473
It was the simplest and easiest
way to fix your problem:

00:02:44.144 --> 00:02:48.083
Hit Control + Alt +
Delete and start all over.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.740
(tape rewinding)

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.729
- [Narrator] Despite all this,
Dr. Dave doesn't think much

00:02:53.729 --> 00:02:56.670
of this contribution to computing history.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.960
- [Dave] I did lots of things with IBM,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.890
but all everybody remembers
is Control + Alt + Delete.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.090
But, I'll take that.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.350
The fame of Control + Alt + Delete

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:09.100
means that I worked on a
very successful product,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.086
and I'm very proud of
having been able to do that.

00:03:12.086 --> 00:03:15.503
(fun instrumental music)

